diff --git a/src/requests/adapters.py.md b/src/requests/adapters.py.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5bf4813
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/requests/adapters.py.md
@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+---
+title: "adapters.py"
+date: 2026-09-23
+hash: "sha256:3fd06509750bffea4dc56fd48e537da70c02ef412651ee9fdf7a197fcaf4e35c"
+---
+## Decisions
+- A session-wide default timeout is `HTTPAdapter(timeout=...)`, mounted on the session. It is not on
+  `Session`. The product owner confirmed this on 2026-09-23 and it follows the standing decision in
+  `sessions.py.md`: a timeout is connection behaviour, which the adapters own. This rules out any
+  `Session.timeout` and any timeout merging in `Session.request()`/`send()`.
+- A call's `timeout=None` counts as "not given", so the adapter default applies. With a default set,
+  `(None, None)` (or a urllib3 `Timeout(connect=None, read=None)`) is how one call waits forever.
+  The adapter cannot tell an omitted timeout from an explicit `None`, because `Session.request()`
+  passes `None` for both. Telling them apart would take a sentinel in `Session`, which is timeout
+  logic in `Session` and ruled out above. This also matches requests' convention that a `None`
+  per-call setting means "unset" (`merge_setting` does the same for verify/stream/cert). It rules out
+  reading an explicit `None` as "wait forever" when a default is set. The user guide's "pass None to
+  wait forever" stays true only for an adapter with no default.
+- A call's timeout replaces the default as a whole, and components are never merged. `(None, 3)`
+  from a call means "no connect timeout". It never means "take the connect timeout from the default".
+  Why: the maintainers' only recorded evidence against session timeouts is that users expected
+  "various different things" from them. A per-component merge makes `None` inside a tuple mean two
+  different things.
